          #a4 ,the print staff

  ,the modern staff comprises five lines
and four spaces4
  ,every line or space on the staff
represents a white key on the keyboard4
  ,in print music1 a note is a round
head placed on a line or in a space4
,the higher the note sits on the staff1
the higher it sounds4
                                      #b
  ,clefs assign individual notes to
certain lines or spaces4
  ,two clefs are normally used3 the
,treble and ,bass clefs4
  ,for the braille reader3 ,braille
music does not use a five-line staff4
,each pitch is a braille cell4 ,an
octave mark before the note tells you
how high or low that pitch is4 ,you
still need the print staff names so you
can follow class and talk about the same
notes as your classmates4

     #b4 ,treble clef1 the ,g clef

  ,first1 we will discuss the ,treble
,clef1 also called the ,g ,clef4
  ,in print1 the treble clef is a large
symbol at the left of the staff4 ,its
curl wraps around line #b1 the second
line from the bottom4 ,that line is ,g4
,any note placed on this line becomes
,g4 ,this ,g is the ,g above middle ,c4
  ,classmates may see that ,g line
                                      #c
highlighted in color4 ,in this file1 it
is named instead of colored4
  ,from that ,g1 letter names move up
the staff in order4 ,the musical
alphabet uses the letters ,a through ,g1
then it starts again at ,a4 ,remember1
there is not an ,h note4
,the note on line #b is ,g4
,the note on the space above ,g is ,a4
,the note on the line above ,a is ,b4
  ,that is line #c4
,the note on the space above ,b is ,c4
,the note on the line above ,c is ,d4
  ,that is line #d4
,the note on the space above ,d is ,e4
,the note on the line above ,e is ,f4
  ,that is line #e1 the top line of the
  staff4
  ,this process continues4

