           #c4 ,ledger lines

  ,uh-oh4 ,we just ran out of room to
place notes4 ,what happens now8
  ,ledger lines will solve our dilemma4
  ,a ledger line is a small extra line
drawn above or below the staff when we
run out of room4 ,it is only as wide as
the note that sits on it4
  ,if we keep going up from the top of
                                      #e
the treble staff3
,the top line is ,f4
,the space above the staff is ,g4
,one ledger line above the staff is ,a4
  ,with the ledger line drawn1 we can
place that ,a4 ,it is the ,a above the
treble staff4
  ,ledger lines also appear below the
staff4 ,one ledger line below the treble
staff is middle ,c4

      #d4 ,bass clef1 the ,f clef

  ,next1 let's discuss the ,bass ,clef1
also called the ,f ,clef4
  ,in print1 the bass clef is a symbol
at the left of the staff with two dots4
,the staff line that sits between those
two dots is ,f4 ,that is line #d1 the
second line from the top4 ,any note on
that line is ,f4 ,this ,f is the ,f
below middle ,c4
  ,we can now fill the rest of the staff
with notes4 ,letter names still run ,a
                                      #f
through ,g1 then start again4

