          #e4 ,the grand staff

  ,finally1 we will discuss the ,grand
,staff1 a theoretical staff consisting
of eleven lines4
                                      #g
  ,imagine eleven lines stacked like one
tall staff4 ,the middle line of those
eleven is middle ,c4
  ,watch what happens when we eliminate
the middle line3 we end up with two
regular five-line staffs1 and a gap
where that middle line was4 ,those two
staffs are also called staves4
  ,by adding a ,treble ,clef to the top
staff and a ,bass ,clef to the bottom
staff1 we can see the relationship
between the two staffs4
  ,notice how the two clefs are joined
by ,c4 ,classmates may see that ,c
marked in color4 ,it sits on a ledger
line in the gap between the two staffs4
,it is one ledger line below the treble
  staff4
,it is one ledger line above the bass
  staff4
,it is the same key on the piano4
  ,this ,c is commonly called middle ,c1
since it corresponds to the middle staff
line on the ,grand ,staff4
                                      #h
  ,from middle ,c1 notes going up are
written in the treble staff4 ,notes
going down are written in the bass
staff4

  #f4 ,how this maps to braille music

  ,you will learn the full ,music
,braille ,code later4 ,for today1
remember this difference4
  ,print music3 the vertical place of a
note on the staff tells you the letter
name and how high or low it is4
  ,braille music3 a note cell tells you
the letter name1 ,a through ,g4 ,an
octave mark before the note tells you
how high or low it is4
  ,there is still no ,h4

